<div class="pk-card" id="p-delete-weekend-flatten-timer-0821" data-id="delete-weekend-flatten-timer-0821" data-default="live" data-pick="1"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">SATURDAY #1 · Front page (how the open position ended); Part 1.6 §5; the unit&#x27;s own journal, which is the arbiter here rather than any desk instrument. · DELETE gazbot7-weekend-flatten.timer — it is still armed and it fires again on Sunday</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play"><code>systemctl disable --now gazbot7-weekend-flatten.timer</code> and remove the unit file. It was built as a one-shot for a position that no longer exists, and it is the only row on this card with a date attached.</div><div class="pk-meta">The unit is written to self-disable once the venue reads flat — but it dies on a <code>PermissionError</code> before it ever reads the venue, so it has never disabled itself. <code>systemctl list-timers</code> has it next elapsing <strong>Sunday 2026-08-30 22:00Z</strong>, then every minute to 23:59Z.</div><details class="pk-det"><summary>why &amp; the full detail</summary><div class="pk-more-why">What it does when it runs is re-arm the day rider and write a <em>bare-stamp</em> claim through the operator-button path — and a bare stamp means <strong>claim everything</strong>. Against whatever happens to be open that night, on a shared netted account, that is the 08-06 cascade with a timer on it. ·  A recovery job that outlives the incident it was built for is not a safety net, it is an unowned actor with write authority. This one cannot even report that it failed: the page() call sits BELOW the file write that kills it. ·  The number: 119 firings on 2026-08-23, 0 orders placed, 0 pages sent. Next elapse Sun 2026-08-30 22:00Z. Journal: 105 gateway failures then 14 PermissionError. ·  Proven wrong by: <code>systemctl list-timers | grep weekend-flatten</code> returns nothing, and <code>systemctl is-enabled gazbot7-weekend-flatten.timer</code> says <code>disabled</code> or the unit is gone.</div></details><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" id="p-day-rider-protective-stop-3atr-0821" data-id="day-rider-protective-stop-3atr-0821" data-default="skip" data-pick="1"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">SATURDAY #2 · Part 1.5 disposition (LIVE) and Part 1.6 §3b — the corrected rider-stop grid, raced on every day rather than only the losing one. · Re-open the day rider&#x27;s protective stop — the 2026-08-20 &ldquo;no stop&rdquo; decision was taken on a number measured on a DIFFERENT rider</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play"><strong>Read the standing decision first, because this play re-opens it.</strong> <code>src/gazbot7/day_rider.py:93</code> carries <code>PLACE_VENUE_STOP = False</code> under, verbatim: <em>&ldquo;★★★ NO STOP. Operator, 2026-08-20: <strong>i dont want any stop. leave them all naked … if its going south my claim profit buttons also work as a manual kill button.</strong>&rdquo;</em> The same block records the exposure as knowingly accepted — median worst-adverse 160pt = −$1,284 on four lots, worst session 1,084pt = −$8,672 — and cites the desk&#x27;s own counter-evidence: a venue stop <em>&ldquo;costs $3,451 of expectancy AND has a WORSE worst-day (−$1,603) than running naked (−$1,531)&rdquo;</em>.</div><div class="pk-meta"><strong>WHAT IS NEW, and why one week does not overturn a 231-session decision.</strong>&lt;br&gt;<strong>(a) The cited counter-evidence is not about this configuration.</strong> Trace the $3,451 back and it lands in <code>day_rider.py</code>&#x27;s own header, where it describes a <strong>400-POINT fixed stop on the 2-LOT rider with the 150pt trail and the 21:00 flat</strong> — the pre-08-20 machine — over 31 validated sessions. It is quoted at line 96 as though it settled the question for the 4-lot ladder.</div><details class="pk-det"><summary>why &amp; the full detail</summary><div class="pk-more-play"><strong>The ask is therefore NOT &ldquo;ship a 3.0×ATR stop&rdquo;. It is: look at the 231-session re-derivation below, then decide.</strong> If you still want it naked, that is a decision this report accepts and stops re-proposing; what it will not do is leave the number unchallenged, because the number turns out to be about a different rider. <strong>If the answer is still no</strong>, the narrower ask that the 08-20 objection does not already answer is a stop that arms <em>only when the flatten path is unhealthy</em> — i.e. place nothing while the gateway answers, and place a wide resting stop the moment <code>ib.connectAsync</code> fails or the heartbeat goes stale. That is insurance against losing the hand, not a trading decision, and it is exactly the framing <code>day_rider.py</code>&#x27;s own header uses for the 600pt stop it already describes.</div><div class="pk-more-why">It does not, and it is not reproducible on it: raced on the 231 lake sessions with the ladder that actually shipped, a 400pt fixed stop scores <strong>−$30,058 against naked&#x27;s −$32,810</strong> and cuts the worst session from −$8,616 to −$3,206. On the machine the operator is actually running, that stop is free and it halves the tail.&lt;br&gt;<strong>(b) The 08-21 carry is new information the decision could not have had.</strong> The objection &ldquo;my claim button IS the kill button&rdquo; assumes a hand at the desk. On 08-21 there was not one for three days: the gateway wedged at 19:57Z, all four automatic flatten paths share one <code>ib.connectAsync</code> and died together, and the position closed by hand on Monday for −$2,149.44. <strong>Every stop width tested closes it by 13:31:55Z</strong>, six and a half hours before the wedge.&lt;br&gt;<strong>THE 231-SESSION RACE, same ladder, same trail, same fee, only the stop changes.</strong> Entry delay is swept 0/2/4/8 minutes per rider_lab&#x27;s standing rule. Naked: −$32,810 / −$29,996 / −$23,597 / −$28,576, worst session −$8,616. 3.0×ATR: −$9,940 / −$5,678 / −$423 / −$2,234, worst session −$1,689. <strong>EVERY stop width beats naked at EVERY delay</strong> — the widths run 1.0× through 5.0× and land in a −$1 to −$52 per session band where naked is −$102 to −$142. That is a plateau on the decision (have a stop) and NOT on the width (nothing separates 1.0× from 3.0× on expectancy). Where the widths do separate cleanly is the tail, monotonically: worst session −$567 at 1.0×, −$848 at 1.5×, −$1,128 at 2.0×, −$1,409 at 2.5×, <strong>−$1,689 at 3.0×</strong>, −$2,251 at 4.0×, −$2,812 at 5.0×, −$8,616 naked.&lt;br&gt;<strong>THE HONEST OTHER SIDE, and it is not small.</strong> Nothing here is profitable: the 4-lot ladder loses money over 231 sessions in every arm including the live one, so this is a choice between losses. And on the ONE session since publication the operator&#x27;s instinct won — on 08-25 the rider went 209pt against a 4-lot long and he claimed at −57pt for −$243.00, where a 3.0×ATR stop would have cut at 14:03:57Z for <strong>−$452.16</strong>, $209 worse. The harness cannot model a hand-kill at all (it only models claiming a WINNER), so the operator&#x27;s own mechanism is untested by every number above. ·  A decision taken on a misattributed number should be re-taken on the right one, and this one was: the $3,451 belongs to a 400pt stop on a 2-lot rider over 31 sessions, not to an ATR stop on the 4-lot ladder over 231. On the real configuration the stop is not a cost, it is a tail cut of roughly 80% for no measurable expectancy give-up. But the operator&#x27;s objection has not been refuted either — it names a mechanism (his hand) that no backtest here can score, and on the single session since this report was drafted that mechanism beat every stop on the grid. ·  The number: 231 sessions, shipped 4-lot ladder: naked −$32,810 (worst session −$8,616); 3.0×ATR −$9,940 (worst session −$1,689); every width beats naked at every entry delay. This week, fee-corrected and with the carry now resolved: as-traded −$2,400.44 across six entries, 3.0×ATR −$532.10. On 08-25, the one session since: as-traded −$243.00, 3.0×ATR −$452.16. ·  Proven wrong by: Whichever way it goes, the decision is recorded IN THE CODE at <code>day_rider.py:93</code> with today&#x27;s date and this section&#x27;s numbers beside the 08-20 quote, so the next reader does not re-litigate it from a 31-session figure. If a stop ships: a resting stop order visible on the venue within 60s of a rider entry and <code>day_rider_state.json</code> carrying its price — not a soft stop in code, because a soft stop dies in the same wedge that caused this.</div></details><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" id="p-standdown-to-a-file-0821" data-id="standdown-to-a-file-0821" data-default="live" data-pick="1"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">SATURDAY #3 · Part 2.6 §4 and its disposition (LIVE, named a Saturday item). · Make the tournament standdown a FILE that every writer checks</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">Move <code>TOURNAMENT_STOOD_DOWN</code> out of <code>router_tick_durable.py</code> and into a state file, and make <code>open_hour_watch.py</code> — and any future writer — read it before writing a switch.</div><div class="pk-meta">The standdown is a router-LOCAL variable. It only stops the ROUTER writing <code>on</code>.</div><details class="pk-det"><summary>why &amp; the full detail</summary><div class="pk-more-why"><code>open_hour_watch.py</code> is a second writer with off→on authority and zero standdown awareness, and it has armed <code>abs_veto_short</code> straight through the standing operator standdown <strong>twice</strong>: 2026-08-21 14:03Z (re-benched 14:05:18Z, 2m15s) and — ★ REV3, found at publication — again on <strong>2026-08-24 13:46:32Z</strong> (re-benched 13:50:22Z, <strong>3m50s</strong>), three days after the report&#x27;s window with nothing changed in between. The only reason either cost nothing is that the tape produced no qualifying signal inside the window, and neither paged. Two $0 outcomes are not a control; they are the same uncontrolled exposure sampled twice, the second one longer. ·  A safety state that one of its two writers cannot see is not a safety state. It has now been breached twice in four days and the exposure got longer, not shorter; both were $0 purely by luck, and luck is not a control. ·  The number: 2 unauthorised arms — 2026-08-21 (2m15s) and 2026-08-24 (3m50s), both $0 by luck, both unpaged. <code>open-hour-watch.service</code>&#x27;s own unit file says it must NEVER write <code>gate_switches.env</code>; it has written since 08-05. ·  Proven wrong by: Set the standdown, then force an <code>open_hour_watch</code> arm condition and confirm it refuses and logs the refusal. A test that cannot be shown to fail is not a test.</div></details><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" id="p-delete-carveout-header-0821" data-id="delete-carveout-header-0821" data-default="live" data-pick="1"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">SATURDAY #4 · Part 2.6 §5 and its disposition (LIVE). · Delete the five expired carve-outs from gate_switches.env&#x27;s comment header</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">Open <code>data/gate_switches.env</code> and delete the five dated instruction lines in its header. Deleting the lines is the only thing that closes a carve-out.</div><div class="pk-meta"><code>switch_notes()</code> splices the top 45 comment lines of that file into EVERY router prompt, labelled ACTIVE, and nothing prunes them. ★ REV3, re-read at publication by importing <code>switch_notes()</code> rather than grepping the file: there are now SIX carve-out blocks and the cap splices FIVE of them (08-24, 08-21, 08-19, 08-13, 08-11), cutting off mid-sentence inside the 11 August one.</div><details class="pk-det"><summary>why &amp; the full detail</summary><div class="pk-more-why">The oldest line still spliced is fifteen days old. Four are past their own stated expiry. <strong>A sixth block was prepended on 2026-08-24 13:46Z and it pushed the 2026-08-09 line — the dangerous one — past the 45-line cap, so it is no longer spliced: <strong>evicted, not closed</strong>, with nothing in the file recording it. That line asserts that <code>abs_veto_short</code> IS ARMED BY DEFAULT</strong> — which directly contradicts the standing operator standdown the router is currently enforcing, on the same gate that <code>open_hour_watch.py</code> armed through that standdown on Friday. ·  A carve-out comment never expires and a zero-mention streak is not a fix. Two of the five have been restated to the router 106 and 66 times out of 1,425 ticks; the other three sit there quietly and would be read the moment the tape produces the situation they describe. ·  The number: 5 instructions spliced as ACTIVE, oldest