<div class="pk-card" id="p-price-violent-whipsaw-bench-0821" data-id="price-violent-whipsaw-bench-0821" data-default="shadow" data-pick="1"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">BUILD #15 · Movement 3 §5 (the chop card) and §10 disposition; the chopscalp dossier §2 and §13 in the appendix. · ★ Price a violent-whipsaw router bench — the chop commission&#x27;s premise was wrong</span><span class="pill v-exploratory">◦ EXPLORATORY</span></div><div class="pk-play">Measure a two-term bench (high ATR + low efficiency) on the tournament&#x27;s own fires, and propose it as a router rule only once it is priced.</div><div class="pk-meta">You asked for a chop-day scalper because the desk donates on chop days. <strong>It does not.</strong> On this week&#x27;s two chop days (08-20 and 08-21) the tournament took ZERO trades, and the week&#x27;s −$2,323 is the day rider&#x27;s book.</div><details class="pk-det"><summary>why &amp; the full detail</summary><div class="pk-more-why">Across the whole 18-day window the tournament&#x27;s loss is concentrated in <strong>violent-whipsaw</strong> blocks — high ATR going nowhere — at <strong>−$1,383.50 on 95 trades</strong>, against −$403.00 on 58 in plain chop. ·  The correction is worth roughly ten times the scalper that was commissioned to fix the wrong thing, and it needs no new instrument — both terms are already in the router&#x27;s vocabulary. ·  The number: Violent-whipsaw −$1,383.50 on 95 trades vs plain chop −$403.00 on 58; zero tournament trades on the week&#x27;s two chop days. ·  Proven wrong by: A table of the tournament&#x27;s P&amp;amp;L by (ATR bucket × ER bucket) over 18 days, with the bench&#x27;s cost in forgone winners priced beside its saving.</div></details><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live" disabled title="Refuted/exploratory — must survive a week in shadow first">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></di